    "kill": {
      "rule": "k=10 recovering < 30% of the oracle gap closes the metal_out line",
      "value": -0.6247,
      "triggered": true,
      "interpretation": "The pre-registered arm fires. The unshrunk mean of the same k rows recovers 0.10 of the gap at k=10 and 0.68 at k=50, so the level is in the rows but ten of them are not enough to read it: the sampling error of a 10-row mean is the same size as the bias it is meant to remove. Two separate defects make the pre-registered estimator worse than the baseline at every k: the paper term is confounded with the training metal's level and does not transfer, and kappa_metal estimated on the observed metals is effectively infinite because the held-out metal sits outside their between-metal variance. Closing the line at k=10 is correct for the deployment story as written; a k~50 protocol would need its own pre-registration."
    }
  },
  "caveats": [
    "The k rows are drawn uniformly from the held-out metal's cohort, not from a designed plate. A deployment that can choose which compounds to measure may do better; that is a different experiment.",
    "The plan specified a two-level decomposition with the metal term identified from within-paper cross-metal residual differences. That estimator is not implemented here because the data cannot identify it: only a small share of k rows belong to papers that also appear in a training metal, and when they do their training rows span several metals, so the difference does not isolate the held-out metal. The share is reported per metal and per k instead of assuming it away.",
    "The paper offset is estimated from the paper's rows in the training metals. Those rows also carry their own metal's level, so the estimate is confounded with a metal effect and does not transfer to the held-out metal; the paper_only arm measures that failure rather than hiding it.",
    "kappa_metal is estimated on the four observed metals. The held-out metal is by construction the one whose level the model gets wrong, so it sits outside that variance and the shrinkage becomes near-total. This is a property of leave-one-metal-out, not of empirical Bayes.",
    "The oracle uses every row of the held-out metal, including the rows a real method would spend on calibration. It is a ceiling, not a model.",
    "Weighted R^2 is the n-weighted mean of per-metal R^2, matching msrs_lomo_surface. It is not a pooled R^2 and the two differ by construction."
  ]
}
